# Lesson 4, new exercise: the lesson's Q95 opposite A82. Test the diamonds first; when they don't split, lead toward dummy's queen.
title: Lead toward the queen
id: L04-05
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.4
student: S
board: 63
dealer: S
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  Q95    Q73    KQ64   A54
  E  JT63   652    J952   Q8
  S  A82    AK4    A73    9632
  W  K74    JT98   T8     KJT7

auction:
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 15 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 8-10
  spades: 1-2 = The ace. Dummy's queen too, if West has the king and you lead toward the queen.
  hearts: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  diamonds: 3-4 = The ace, king and queen. A fourth if they split 3-3.
  clubs: 1 = The ace.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: You have two chances for a ninth trick. Which can you try without giving up the lead?
play:
  1   W HJ  N H3  E H2  S HK
      note: Eight winners on top. The ninth could come from a 3-3 diamond split, or from dummy's ♠Q if West has the king. Win in your hand.
  2   S DA  W D8  N D4  E D2
      note: Try the diamonds first: if they split 3-3 you have nine tricks without any risk. Your ace first, the high card from the short side.
  3   S D3  W DT  N DK  E D5
      note: Over to dummy's king and queen.
  4   N DQ  E D9  S D7  W C7
      note: West shows out, so East still has the ♦J. No fourth diamond trick. Time for the second chance.
  5   N H7  E H5  S HA  W H8
      note: Back to your hand, so you can lead a spade toward dummy's queen.
  6   S S2  W S4  N SQ*  E S3
      note: Lead a low spade toward the queen.
      SQ key: West played low, so play the queen. If West has the king, the queen wins.
      SQ not *: Did you think about playing the queen? If West has the king, it can't beat the queen, because West has already played.
  7   N HQ  E H6  S H4  W H9
      note: The queen won. Now cash dummy's ♥Q.
  8   N S5  E S6  S SA  W S7
      note: Then your ♠A.
  9   S C2  W CT  N CA  E C8
      note: And the ♣A. That's nine tricks.
  10  N D6  E DJ  S C6  W HT
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  11  E ST  S S8  W SK  N S9
  12  W CJ  N C4  E CQ  S C9
  13  E SJ  S C3  W CK  N C5
      note: 9 tricks: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: count eight winners on top, then look for the ninth. Try the
  diamonds first, because that chance costs nothing. When they don't split,
  lead a spade toward dummy's queen.

  Leading toward the queen wins whenever West has the king: West must play
  before dummy. Leading the queen from dummy would not work: whoever has the
  king would simply cover it. Here the king was with West. If East had held
  it, the queen would have lost, and you would have gone down one.
